\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Setup, in order\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1. Accounts and keys (15 minutes, do first)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Telegram bot token
\f1\b0  \'97 message 
\f2\fs26 @BotFather
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 /newbot
\f1\fs24 , keep the token.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your Telegram user ID
\f1\b0  \'97 message 
\f2\fs26 @userinfobot
\f1\fs24 . Goes in 
\f2\fs26 ALLOWED_TELEGRAM_IDS
\f1\fs24 . Without it, anyone who finds your bot is billed to your API key.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Anthropic API key
\f1\b0  \'97 console.anthropic.com. Set a spend limit on it. The architecture puts you at roughly $0.42/month; a limit of $20 catches a runaway loop long before it matters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Local first, VPS second\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Develop on the Mac against a local Postgres in Docker. Deploy to the VPS only once it works locally. Debugging a container on a remote box over SSH is the slowest possible way to find an asyncpg type-coercion error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 cd ~/Workspace && unzip nutribot.zip && cd nutribot\
git init && git add -A && git commit -m "scaffold"\
cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env\
make db          # Postgres 16, sql/*.sql applies on first boot\
make test        # 60 tests, no DB needed \'97 confirms nothing broke in transit\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Download the FoodData Central full CSV export from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/download-datasets"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/download-datasets}} (public domain, CC0 1.0), then:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 make load FDC=~/Downloads/FoodData_Central_csv_2026-XX-XX\
make bootstrap ID=<your telegram id>\
make run\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Message the bot 
\f2\fs26 black coffee
\f1\fs24 . That is the whole first milestone.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3. The VPS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A \'805\'9610/month shared-vCPU box is ample: 2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM, 40 GB disk. Postgres with Foundation + SR Legacy + FNDDS loaded is well under 2 GB. Hetzner's CX/CPX line is the obvious pick from Warsaw for latency and price, though I could not retrieve their current published figures \'97 check {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hetzner.com/cloud/pricing/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://www.hetzner.com/cloud/pricing/}} before committing.\
Ubuntu 24.04, Docker, a non-root user, SSH keys only, 
\f2\fs26 ufw
\f1\fs24  allowing 22 only. The bot uses long polling, so 
\f0\b no inbound ports and no TLS certificate are needed
\f1\b0  \'97 it dials out to Telegram. Postgres has no published port in 
\f2\fs26 docker-compose.yml
\f1\fs24  deliberately.\
Deploy is 
\f2\fs26 git pull && docker compose build bot && docker compose up -d bot
\f1\fs24 , wrapped as 
\f2\fs26 make deploy
\f1\fs24 . The database volume is untouched by it.\
Take a 
\f2\fs26 pg_dump
\f1\fs24  on a cron before you have a month of data you would miss.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
